Superficial friction abrasion (gravel rash) due to fall from (personal transport equipment) to elbow
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Raw olecranon abrasion, Embedded street debris, High-speed slide mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-032
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe friction wound with embedded debris following a scooter fall, requiring urgent pain relief and decontamination.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a high-speed slide on asphalt resulting in a severely painful, dirty friction burn over a major joint. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to aggressively irrigate the wound, clear the joint of structural damage, and apply specialized occlusive dressings to prevent severe staphylococcal infection and manage neuropathic pain before daytime clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I came off my scooter and slid on the road; my elbow is completely raw, full of gravel, and burning uncontrollably.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a clear history of a high-speed slide on hardscape after falling from a motorized scooter 1 hour ago. This resulted in a broad, raw, denuded area of skin overlying the olecranon process and posterior elbow. The patient reports intense localized burning pain accompanied by extreme tenderness to light tactile stimuli and ambient air. There is weeping serosanguinous fluid pooling constantly on the exposed wound surface. The patient notes macroscopic dirt and small asphalt particles embedded within the superficial dermal layers.
Functional Status: Fully independent; holding the arm slightly flexed to avoid stretching the damaged skin.
Social/Safety Concerns: Operating personal transport equipment without protective joint pads.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 136/86 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, in moderate acute distress related to exposed nerve endings, visibly protecting the affected elbow.
Physical Exam: Upper limb examination reveals a raw olecranon abrasion with heavily embedded street debris. There is a total absence of a deep laceration penetrating into the olecranon bursa or the periosteum. Full active range of motion of the elbow is present, albeit limited slightly by discomfort from skin stretching. Normal triceps and biceps motor strength testing is confirmed. Ulnar nerve function is intact, demonstrating normal sensation in the ipsilateral small and ring fingers. Capillary refill is normal distally.
Environmental Observations: Helmet utilized and intact; no elbow pads worn.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial friction abrasion to elbow - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Olecranon fracture - Ruled out due to: Preserved, strong active extension against gravity and absence of localized bony step-off or severe bony point tenderness.

Traumatic olecranon bursitis (open) - Ruled out due to: Visual confirmation that the dermal defect does not penetrate into the bursa sac; no synovial fluid leakage.

Ulnar nerve contusion - Ruled out due to: Completely normal sensation in the 4th and 5th digits and intact intrinsic hand motor function.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

High-velocity friction injuries over the elbow carry a high risk of traumatic bursal penetration and severe retained foreign body infection. Bedside clinical assessment confidently excluded deep structural damage and joint penetration, isolating the injury to extensive epidermal loss. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to execute highly painful but necessary mechanical scrubbing of asphalt debris under local anesthetic gel, and to apply an immediate occlusive dressing to terminate the severe neuropathic pain cycle, averting permanent traumatic tattooing and infection.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Applied topical anesthetic gel. Performed rigorous high-volume syringe irrigation and mechanical gauze scrubbing with chlorhexidine to extract all macroscopic gravel. Applied a non-adherent silicone foam occlusive dressing. Tetanus prophylaxis updated.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the silicone dressing sealed tight for 4 to 5 days without peeling it back. This traps the body's natural healing fluids in a dark, moist environment, which completely stops the burning pain and forces the skin to heal twice as fast as leaving it open to the air.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Silicone foam dressings, tubular retention bandage.
Follow-up: Routine GP or practice nurse review in 4 days for a scheduled dressing change and wound inspection.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if the elbow becomes massively swollen, hot to the touch, red streaks travel up the arm, or if you develop a systemic fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Friction burns naturally produce a large amount of yellow fluid during the first 48 hours; this is not necessarily pus.
Clinical Course: The sharp burning pain will cease within hours under the dressing. Full epithelialization of this large area will take 10 to 14 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if you suddenly cannot bend or straighten your elbow at all, or if your pinky finger becomes completely numb.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Asphalt creates a dirty, raw wound that leaves nerve endings completely exposed. The specialized dressing acts as artificial skin, immediately blocking the air from hitting the nerves and preventing infection.
Actionable Steps: Do not rip the dressing off, even if it bubbles up with fluid. Keep the arm protected in the shower. Wear elbow pads when riding your scooter.

